// Horror and Comedy together: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== horror+comedy.x01 | feature | three-act
title: The Understudy
genre: horror + comedy
tone: Draughty, sincere and increasingly frightened; amateur dramatics played straight
era: - | contemporary
setting: Little Morden Village Hall, Suffolk | fictional | village
logline: A village drama society discovers that the ghost haunting their hall only appears when the acting is bad, and their Christmas production is three weeks away.
premise: The Little Morden Players have lost their hall's heating, half their cast and their director. {gwen}, the treasurer, takes over a creaking country-house mystery because nobody else will. At the first rehearsal a cold figure in an Edwardian stage-manager's apron walks through the back wall during {barnaby}'s big speech. It does not come when {ines}, the only one who can act, is speaking. It comes more solidly every time someone forgets a line.
synopsis: The first act is a comedy of denial. {gwen} has a budget of two hundred and eleven pounds and a cast who think projection means shouting. The thing in the apron is seen first by {dot} on the prompt book, who says nothing because she assumes it is someone's husband. By the third rehearsal it has a name, found on a 1911 programme in the props cupboard: {thrale}, stage manager, who died in the wings during a performance so poor that the parish magazine apologised for it. It is drawn to bad acting as to an open wound. A dropped cue brings a chill; a wooden line brings footsteps; {barnaby}'s death scene, which he plays every night as if being stung, brings the whole figure to the edge of the stage, where it stands with its hand out for the script. The second act stops being funny by degrees. {ines}, a hospice nurse who joined to have one evening a week that was not about dying, works out the rule and what it means: they can leave the hall, cancel the play and let the society fold after ninety years, or they can get good. {gwen} chooses the play. What follows is the oddest rehearsal process ever put on film: terror as a director. They learn their lines because not knowing them is cold. {barnaby} is taught, cruelly and then kindly, to say one sentence as if he meant it. {dot} confesses she has prompted from the wrong edition for eleven years. {thrale} is not appeased by effort; only by truth, and he can tell the difference. At the dress rehearsal {barnaby} dries completely, and the figure walks on, takes his place and begins to play the part itself, beautifully, in a voice like a door in another house, and the hall goes so cold the fire-exit sign cracks. It will not leave the stage. A ghost that has waited a century for a competent production has decided to give one. The third act is opening night with a full house who think the cold is the heating. The Players must act their own play around a dead man who is better than all of them and take it back from him line by line: not by being as good, but by meaning it. {ines} carries the first half. {gwen}, who has never acted, goes on in the second for a cast member who has fled, and is terrible, and the figure turns on her. What saves her is {barnaby}, who for the first time in his life listens to another actor and answers her as though she had actually spoken. It is one honest exchange between two frightened people in bad wigs. {thrale} stops. He looks at the prompt corner. {dot} gives him his cue from the right edition: "House lights, Mr. Thrale." He nods once, and calls the curtain, and is not there. The review in the parish magazine says the second half dragged.
question: Can ordinary people be frightened into being truthful, and does it count if they are?
themes: sincerity against polish; what amateurs are for; the dead as an audience
audience: Adults and older teenagers who like to laugh and then stop
viewpoint: With {gwen}; with {ines} when the rule is being worked out
stakes: A ninety-year-old society, the hall it pays for, {barnaby}'s dignity, and what happens to a stage that a ghost will not give back
conflict: A cast with no talent against a spectre with standards, and {gwen}'s belief that turning up is enough against the discovery that it is not
ending: The Players take the play back with one true exchange. {thrale} is given his cue and calls his last curtain. The society survives; the review is mixed.
experience: Real laughs that curdle into real cold, then a swell of fondness for people being bad at something bravely
look: Stacked chairs, a painted flat of a library, tungsten work lights; breath showing indoors; the figure always part-hidden by a flat or a curtain until the dress rehearsal
sound: A hall's echo, a wall heater that ticks and gives nothing, lines learned aloud in cars. No music but the Players' own interval tape.
m: keep | creature | ambition-vs-integrity | bittersweet
rule: It comes when a line is said falsely | Nothing happens while someone is speaking truthfully, however badly
rule: It keeps to the stage and the wings | The car park, the kitchen and the lavatories are safe, and everyone knows it
@ gwen | Gwen Aldous | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 58 | To get one more production on | The society is the only thing in the village older than the bypass | She has never directed, cannot act and has three weeks | Organised to a fault and blind to how afraid she is | From believing turning up is enough to going on herself | Brisk, apologetic, treasurer's vocabulary | Lanyard with the hall keys, body warmer, a float tin
@ ines | Ines Carvalho | co-lead | deuteragonist | human | she/her | 34 | One evening a week that is not about dying | She is a hospice nurse and very good at it | The thing in the hall is exactly her day job | Truthful by habit, impatient with fuss | From wanting an escape to using what she knows | Quiet, direct, lets a pause do the work | Scrubs under a coat, script covered in neat pencil
@ barnaby | Barnaby Sutch | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 67 | To be thought good | He has had the lead for twenty-two years because he owns the costume | He has never once listened on a stage | Vain, generous, terrified of silence | From declaiming at people to answering one | Booming, then, once, small | A cravat in all weathers, his own smoking jacket
@ dot | Dorothy "Dot" Pym | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 79 | To be useful from the corner | She has been on the book since 1968 | The wrong edition, and her eyes | Serene, deaf on one side, unshockable | From assuming it is someone's husband to giving a dead man his cue | Whispers that carry further than the acting | Cardigan, a torch on a string, two pairs of glasses
@ thrale | Mr. Thrale | supporting | antagonist | undead | he/him | - | A performance fit to call the curtain on | He died in the wings of a bad one | He cannot be acted at, only acted with | Exacting, courteous, pitiless about falseness | From taking the play to being released from it | Almost nothing; when he plays the part, the best voice in the film | Edwardian apron, sleeve garters, a stub of pencil behind the ear
~ gwen - ines | new director and the only actor she has | {gwen} leans on her | {ines} hands her the second half
~ gwen - barnaby | treasurer and leading man | she manages him | he saves her by listening
~ barnaby - thrale | the worst actor in the hall and its harshest critic | a hand held out for the script | a nod
~ dot - thrale | prompter and stage manager, a century apart | she takes him for a visitor | she gives him his cue
~ ines - thrale | a nurse and someone who has not finished | she recognises it | she tells the others what he needs
